{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab4e3c74801132b7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014 (Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment and gap inventory | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Publish Llama Stack API stability guarantee and close GA-bar gaps | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Establish Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Support TrustyAI guardrails integration for Llama Stack GA | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Enable Llama Stack management in the Dashboard by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Uplift Llama Stack documentation to GA standards | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n22\t\n23\tNotes on the epic list:\n24\t\n25\t- **Teams** are not stated in the strategy (no Impacted Teams table); they are taken from the architecture-context owning teams (`rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md` → RHOAI GenAI, `rhoai-3.0/trustyai.md` → TrustyAI, `rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md` → RHOAI Dashboard).\n26\t- **E004 component span**: the guardrails work touches both TrustyAI and the Llama Stack safety-provider surface. It is assigned the single most-relevant canonical component `TrustyAI` (the integration is graduated on the TrustyAI side per the architecture context, which already lists guardrails under TrustyAI's role); the Llama Stack-side surface is coordinated, not duplicated.\n27\t- **E006 priority**: the docs epic carries P0 via the `docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not via priority inheritance from HLR-6 (P2).\n28\t- All three affected components exist in architecture context — no onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is needed; Llama Stack is already Konflux-onboarded per `rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`.\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability guarantee + GA hardening\"]\n35\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Production support posture\"]\n36\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 GA documentation uplift\"]\n37\t    E003 --> E006\n38\t    E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"] --> E006\n39\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard enable-by-default\"] --> E006\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\t## DAG Justification\n43\t\n44\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n45\t|------|------|-----------|\n46\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The strategy's only open question states the GA scope depends on the assessment — until it lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. The assessment determines what E002 remediates (true gate: `rewrite`). Outcomes are unbounded (a full gap inventory, not ≤3 bounded alternatives), so no conditional branch epics; the inventory itself is the branch selector. |\n47\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets must reflect the assessment's scale-limit and error-handling findings; a different gap severity rewrites the posture content (true gate: `rewrite`). |\n48\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation and TP migration notes describe the stability guarantee, deprecation policy, and tested upgrade paths this epic produces. |\n49\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA-standard documentation surfaces the support posture (SLOs, backport policy, supported configuration matrix). |\n50\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration must be documented as supported, which requires the supported integration to exist. |\n51\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation describes the default-on Dashboard experience for entitled clusters. |\n52\t| No edge E004 ↔ E002 | Rule 14/15 (parallel) | Different components, teams, and repos; the strategy frames HLR-4 as graduating an existing integration, not building against new APIs. The shared safety-provider surface is a coordination risk (see Health Warnings), not a dependency. |\n53\t| No edge E005 ↔ others | Rule 14 (parallel) | The Dashboard default flip is an independent change to the Dashboard's feature-flag defaults; nothing else builds on it before docs. |\n54\t| No edge E004/E005 → E001 | — | Neither the guardrails graduation nor the Dashboard default is scoped by the assessment; both are fully specified by their HLRs and run in parallel from day one. |\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n57\t\n58\t| HLR | Priority | Epic |\n59\t|-----|----------|------|\n60\t| HLR-1 — GA-readiness assessment producing definitive GA scope | P0 | E001 |\n61\t| HLR-2 — API stability guarantee, versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | P0 | E002 |\n62\t| HLR-3 — Production support posture (SLOs, backport policy, config matrix) | P0 | E003 |\n63\t| HLR-4 — TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | P1 | E004 |\n64\t| HLR-5 — Dashboard Llama Stack management on by default for entitled clusters | P1 | E005 |\n65\t| HLR-6 — Documentation uplift to GA standards incl. TP migration notes | P2 | E006 (priority P0 via docs-authoring exception, strategy priority Critical) |\n66\t\n67\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No redundant/overlapping HLR pairs were found (each HLR names a distinct capability and surface).\n68\t\n69\t## Health Warnings\n70\t\n71\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking)**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture-context owning teams; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR text. If the strategy author supplies team or dependency data, E004's team assignment and the E004↔E002 parallelism should be re-checked first.\n72\t2. **E002 size is unestimable until E001 lands (over-guideline management)**: the strategy itself states the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate until the assessment lands, so E002's effort currently sits above the ~2-week epic guideline by admission. Kept whole because GA-bar hardening is one logical change over the same published API surface, and pre-splitting would duplicate the assessment's job (the inventory is the split axis). Re-split trigger: once the assessment lands, if the scoped hardening exceeds the ~2-week guideline, split E002 by the assessment's gap categories (API stability / upgrade path / error handling / scale limits) along the inventory's own boundaries.\n73\t3. **Docs priority label**: E006 shows P0 while its HLR-6 is P2 — this is the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical), not a priority inversion. Flagging so a reviewer does not \"fix\" it downward or read it as urgency to start docs early; Rule 11 still forces it last.\n74\t4. **Coordination risk, not a dependency**: E004 (TrustyAI guardrails integration) and E002 (Llama Stack API stabilization) share the safety-provider integration surface but run in parallel per Rule 14. If E002's API versioning changes the safety surface, E004's integration validation must be re-run — tracked as an acceptance criterion on E004 (validation against the GA distribution), not as a DAG edge.\n75\t5. **Scope trap guard**: out-of-scope explicitly excludes new Llama Stack feature development — E002's remediation scope must stay within GA-bar quality work identified by the assessment, and any assessment finding that amounts to new feature work should be routed back to the strategy, not absorbed into E002.\n76\t\n77\t## Tiered Delivery\n78\t\n79\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing; the gating unknown (the GA scope) is internal to the strategy and resolved by E001. E004 and E005 deliver independent partial value in parallel while the assessment runs, which is the natural sequencing already expressed by the DAG rather than a formal tier split.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad00b49876d2af47","agent_id":"a9e7719b13d5658cf","session_id":"514c5544-223d-42a7-ba52-fa0d432c2321","uuid":"6206fd89-69d0-4b9b-97f9-30b0ec5a0339","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:50:02.188Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab4e3c74801132b7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
