 surface: \"Documentation of the TrustyAI guardrails integration as a supported configuration.\" Two epics owned by different teams (TrustyAI and RHOAI GenAI) independently claim the same deliverable — the boundary was drawn incorrectly.\n\nNamed correction: remove the documentation deliverable from E004's Scope and Acceptance Criteria (keep the code, the experimental-gate removal, and the validation evidence; the Llama Stack owning-team review of the guardrails documentation remains as coordination on E006's side), leaving all guardrails documentation in E006. HLR-4's \"supported, documented integration\" wording stays fully covered because E006 already delivers the documentation and depends on E004. No split of E004 itself is demanded — only the deletion of its duplicated documentation scope.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the ratified GA scope) determines what E002 remediates and what E003's config matrix contains — both downstream epics declare `gated_by: E001` with `gate_failure_impact` populated (action `rewrite` plus a concrete fallback approach), and the gated_by target appears in each epic's direct `dependencies`. Unbounded outcomes correctly produce no conditional branch files. E004 and E005 are fully specified by their HLRs and correctly carry no gates and no assessment edge. E006 has no Investigation dependency and no gate. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal fields are present on every epic (`investigation_signals` on E001, `ai_signals` on E002-E006) — no cross-type scoring errors. Frontmatter values match the rationale files line-for-line on all six epics. E001's investigation signals are consistent with the strategy (scale-limit verification needs a cluster → cluster_hardware_dependence -1; ratification is peripheral to producing the inventory → human_judgment_required -1). `architecture_claims: 1` rationales on E002-E006 cite `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`, `trustyai.md`, and `dashboard.md`, all of which exist — the strategy's TP-maturity premise is corroborated, so calibration is correct. E004's `open_questions: 1` is defensible: the epic is fully specified by HLR-4 and is not scoped by the GA assessment.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text (the strategy has no Given/When/Then section — noted in Health Warning 1). Rule-mandated ACs: E006 (docs-authoring) carries the technical-review AC (\"technically reviewed against the implemented GA capabilities ... by the owning teams\") — present. No konflux chain exists, so no build-pipeline AC applies. E002's upgrade-path AC requires test evidence — testable. No soft-ACs: E003's \"reviewed and accepted by support stakeholders\" is a condition on the epic's own deliverable (driving the review is in the epic's scope), not an externally-granted availability precondition. E005's default flip is delivered through the existing feature-flag mechanism, which is inherently the rollback path. No bare draft-ID references (E001-E006) appear in any epic body. One minor nit: E001's third AC contains a typo, \"support-postature\" for \"support-posture\" — cosmetic, does not affect testability.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: the GA-readiness assessment (E001), API stability guarantee with versioning/deprecation/upgrade path (E002), support posture (E003), guardrails graduation (E004), Dashboard default (E005), and docs uplift with TP migration notes (E006). The strategy's single open question is resolved by design (E001 exists to answer it). Out-of-scope constraints are enforced — E002's scope explicitly excludes new-feature work and routes assessment findings that amount to features back to the strategy (Health Warning 5). Strategy-level context (missing sections, size uncertainty, coordination risk, docs priority label) is carried forward in Health Warnings rather than silently dropped. The strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, but HLR-6's documentation requirement is covered by the Rule-11-compliant E006 regardless.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":30818,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad00b49876d2af47","agent_id":"a9e7719b13d5658cf","session_id":"514c5544-223d-42a7-ba52-fa0d432c2321","uuid":"c7a225b3-00e6-4d74-96f6-7ee3147f0618","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:52:35.509Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
