{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a87b10aa288b1f1b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E004 bundles code implementation (experimental-gate removal, integration\n10\t    validation) with content authoring: its scope item to document the supported integration\n11\t    (configuration, capabilities, support expectations) and the matching AC. Work-product\n12\t    type mixing — remove the documentation deliverable from E004 Scope/ACs and leave\n13\t    all guardrails documentation in E006.'\n14\t- severity: major\n15\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n16\t  description: 'Scope duplication: E004 (TrustyAI team) and E006 (RHOAI GenAI, docs-authoring,\n17\t    already depending on E004) both scope the same documentation surface — the TrustyAI\n18\t    guardrails integration documented as a supported configuration. Fold the documentation\n19\t    wholly into E006 so HLR-4 stays covered via E006; E004 keeps code and validation\n20\t    evidence, with the Llama Stack-team review remaining as coordination on E006 side.'\n21\t- severity: minor\n22\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n23\t  description: 'E001 third acceptance criterion contains a typo: support-postature\n24\t    should read support-posture. Cosmetic; does not affect testability.'\n25\terror: null\n26\t---\n27\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9014\n28\t\n29\t## Review Summary\n30\t\n31\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n32\tRecommendation: revise\n33\t\n34\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — epic body sections, canonical component names, docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, parallelism rules, and shared-surface enforcement all pass).\n35\t\n36\tThe decomposition is strong on coverage, DAG construction, gating metadata, and signal calibration. It fails on one boundary defect: E004 bundles code implementation with documentation content authoring, and that same documentation deliverable is independently scoped in E006 — the guardrails documentation surface is claimed by two epics owned by different teams.\n37\t\n38\t## Criterion Details\n39\t\n40\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n41\tAll six HLRs map to epics (HLR-1→E001, HLR-2→E002, HLR-3→E003, HLR-4→E004, HLR-5→E005, HLR-6→E006); the traceability matrix is present and consistent with epic-level HLR Traceability sections. P2 HLR-6 is delivered (not deferred) via E006. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) gates only P0 work (E002, E003). E006's P0 label is the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0), correctly applied and explicitly explained in the summary — not flagged. No priority collapse: each epic maps to HLRs at a single priority level, and E002's secondary HLR-1 reference is downstream consumption of E001's deliverable, not a bundled deferrable feature.\n42\t\n43\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n44\tAll six edges in the summary diagram match the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies` exactly, in both directions (E001→E002, E001→E003, E002→E006, E003→E006, E004→E006, E005→E006). Arrows run dependency→dependent per convention. No circular or invalid edges; no branch files exist (and none are referenced by main-plan epics). Critical path length 3 (E001→E002→E006) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4). Rule 11 docs-last is satisfied: E006 is blocked by all four implementation epics. No onboarding-chain epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 are N/A. The absence of an E002→E004 edge is correct per Rule 14 (different components, teams, and repos): E004's consumption of the GA distribution's safety-provider surface is a validation/re-validation condition, correctly carried as an AC on E004 rather than a DAG edge — this is the rule-compliant shape for a surface consumed as a verification target, not an artifact dependency, and the summary's DAG Justification and Health Warning 4 record the reasoning.\n45\t\n46\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n47\tComponent/team tuples are clean (Llama Stack/RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI/TrustyAI, Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard, matching architecture-context owning teams); E004's cross-surface span is reasonably assigned to its most-relevant canonical component. E002's over-guideline size is a defensible shape-(b) trade-off: the estimate, why-not-split rationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger are all recorded in Health Warning 2 — recorded as a watch item, not docked. However, two related major defects:\n48\t\n49\t1. **Work-product type mixing in E004.** E004 bundles code implementation (remove the experimental/opt-in gating, validate the integration) with content authoring — its third scope bullet, \"Document the supported integration (configuration, capabilities, and support expectations),\" and its third AC, \"The integration is documented as a supported configuration.\" These are different kinds of work with different review criteria and belong in separate epics even under one team.\n50\t2. **Scope duplication between E004 and E006.** E006 (the docs-authoring epic, which already depends on E004) scopes the identical documentation surface: \"Documentation of the TrustyAI guardrails integration as a supported configuration.\" Two epics owned by different teams (TrustyAI and RHOAI GenAI) independently claim the same deliverable — the boundary was drawn incorrectly.\n51\t\n52\tNamed correction: remove the documentation deliverable from E004's Scope and Acceptance Criteria (keep the code, the experimental-gate removal, and the validation evidence; the Llama Stack owning-team review of the guardrails documentation remains as coordination on E006's side), leaving all guardrails documentation in E006. HLR-4's \"supported, documented integration\" wording stays fully covered because E006 already delivers the documentation and depends on E004. No split of E004 itself is demanded — only the deletion of its duplicated documentation scope.\n53\t\n54\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n55\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the ratified GA scope) determines what E002 remediates and what E003's config matrix contains — both downstream epics declare `gated_by: E001` with `gate_failure_impact` populated (action `rewrite` plus a concrete fallback approach), and the gated_by target appears in each epic's direct `dependencies`. Unbounded outcomes correctly produce no conditional branch files. E004 and E005 are fully specified by their HLRs and correctly carry no gates and no assessment edge. E006 has no Investigation dependency and no gate. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n56\t\n57\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n58\tType-appropriate signal fields are present on every epic (`investigation_signals` on E001, `ai_signals` on E002-E006) — no cross-type scoring errors. Frontmatter values match the rationale files line-for-line on all six epics. E001's investigation signals are consistent with the strategy (scale-limit verification needs a cluster → cluster_hardware_dependence -1; ratification is peripheral to producing the inventory → human_judgment_required -1). `architecture_claims: 1` rationales on E002-E006 cite `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`, `trustyai.md`, and `dashboard.md`, all of which exist — the strategy's TP-maturity premise is corroborated, so calibration is correct. E004's `open_questions: 1` is defensible: the epic is fully specified by HLR-4 and is not scoped by the GA assessment.\n59\t\n60\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n61\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text (the strategy has no Given/When/Then section — noted in Health Warning 1). Rule-mandated ACs: E006 (docs-authoring) carries the technical-review AC (\"technically reviewed against the implemented GA capabilities ... by the owning teams\") — present. No konflux chain exists, so no build-pipeline AC 