{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f0c288ad28397f7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014 (Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability)\n10\t\n11\t## Epic List\n12\t\n13\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n14\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n15\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment and gap inventory | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Publish Llama Stack API stability guarantee and close GA-bar gaps | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Establish Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Support TrustyAI guardrails integration for Llama Stack GA | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Enable Llama Stack management in the Dashboard by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Uplift Llama Stack documentation to GA standards | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n21\t\n22\tNotes on the epic list:\n23\t\n24\t- **Teams** are not stated in the strategy (no Impacted Teams table); they are taken from the architecture-context owning teams (`rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md` → RHOAI GenAI, `rhoai-3.0/trustyai.md` → TrustyAI, `rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md` → RHOAI Dashboard).\n25\t- **E004 component span**: the guardrails work touches both TrustyAI and the Llama Stack safety-provider surface. It is assigned the single most-relevant canonical component `TrustyAI` (the integration is graduated on the TrustyAI side per the architecture context, which already lists guardrails under TrustyAI's role); the Llama Stack-side surface is coordinated, not duplicated.\n26\t- **E006 priority**: the docs epic carries P0 via the `docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not via priority inheritance from HLR-6 (P2).\n27\t- All three affected components exist in architecture context — no onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is needed; Llama Stack is already Konflux-onboarded per `rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`.\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability guarantee + GA hardening\"]\n34\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Production support posture\"]\n35\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 GA documentation uplift\"]\n36\t    E003 --> E006\n37\t    E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"] --> E006\n38\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard enable-by-default\"] --> E006\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The strategy's only open question states the GA scope depends on the assessment — until it lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. The assessment determines what E002 remediates (true gate: `rewrite`). Outcomes are unbounded (a full gap inventory, not ≤3 bounded alternatives), so no conditional branch epics; the inventory itself is the branch selector. |\n46\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets must reflect the assessment's scale-limit and error-handling findings; a different gap severity rewrites the posture content (true gate: `rewrite`). |\n47\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation and TP migration notes describe the stability guarantee, deprecation policy, and tested upgrade paths this epic produces. |\n48\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA-standard documentation surfaces the support posture (SLOs, backport policy, supported configuration matrix). |\n49\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration must be documented as supported, which requires the supported integration to exist. |\n50\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation describes the default-on Dashboard experience for entitled clusters. |\n51\t| No edge E004 ↔ E002 | Rule 14/15 (parallel) | Different components, teams, and repos; the strategy frames HLR-4 as graduating an existing integration, not building against new APIs. The shared safety-provider surface is a coordination risk (see Health Warnings), not a dependency. |\n52\t| No edge E005 ↔ others | Rule 14 (parallel) | The Dashboard default flip is an independent change to the Dashboard's feature-flag defaults; nothing else builds on it before docs. |\n53\t| No edge E004/E005 → E001 | — | Neither the guardrails graduation nor the Dashboard default is scoped by the assessment; both are fully specified by their HLRs and run in parallel from day one. |\n54\t\n55\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n56\t\n57\t| HLR | Priority | Epic |\n58\t|-----|----------|------|\n59\t| HLR-1 — GA-readiness assessment producing definitive GA scope | P0 | E001 |\n60\t| HLR-2 — API stability guarantee, versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | P0 | E002 |\n61\t| HLR-3 — Production support posture (SLOs, backport policy, config matrix) | P0 | E003 |\n62\t| HLR-4 — TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | P1 | E004 |\n63\t| HLR-5 — Dashboard Llama Stack management on by default for entitled clusters | P1 | E005 |\n64\t| HLR-6 — Documentation uplift to GA standards incl. TP migration notes | P2 | E006 (priority P0 via docs-authoring exception, strategy priority Critical) |\n65\t\n66\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No redundant/overlapping HLR pairs were found (each HLR names a distinct capability and surface).\n67\t\n68\t## Health Warnings\n69\t\n70\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking)**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table